          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Beginnings and Early Influences</strong><br>\nFrancis Ford Coppola was born on April 7, 1939, in Detroit, Michigan, and grew up in a creative family with strong ties to music and theatre. He pursued filmmaking at UCLA and began his career with low-budget films and screenwriting, showcasing an early talent for rich storytelling.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise with \"The Godfather\"</strong><br>\nCoppola rose to prominence with \"The Godfather\" in 1972, a film that has since become iconic for its portrayal of the Mafia in America. The film's success established Coppola as a major director of his era and went on to win three Academy Awards, including Best Picture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>A String of Classics</strong><br>\nFollowing \"The Godfather,\" Coppola directed several more classics, including \"The Godfather Part II,\" which became the first sequel to win Best Picture, and \"Apocalypse Now,\" which provided a stark and hallucinatory vision of the Vietnam War.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
